# SaMi AI environment configuration

SaMi AI never hardcodes a model ID.

For normal SaMi operation, provider selection is automatic from the
active provider-specific model variable. API keys are credentials that
you configure once; after that, changing the active AI is normally just
a model change.

## Recommended configuration

# Provider credentials — configure the providers you actually have.
GROQ_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# Active model — normally keep exactly ONE provider model active.
# SaMi infers the provider from this model variable.
GROQ_MODEL=
# OPENAI_MODEL=
# GEMINI_MODEL=
# GOOGLE_MODEL=

SAMI_AI_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=20
SAMI_AI_REQUESTS_PER_DAY=500
SAMI_AI_MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS=6
SAMI_AI_CONTEXT_MESSAGES=24

## Switching providers

To switch from Groq to OpenAI, for example, keep both API keys if you
want, but move the active model:

# GROQ_MODEL=
OPENAI_MODEL=<openai-model-id>

To switch to Gemini:

OPENAI_MODEL=
GEMINI_MODEL=<gemini-model-id>

SaMi will reject an ambiguous setup where more than one provider model
is active at the same time. This prevents an accidental provider/model
mismatch.

## Compatibility / advanced overrides

These remain supported for deployments that need an explicit provider
or another OpenAI-compatible endpoint, but they are not required for
normal Groq/OpenAI/Gemini switching:

SAMI_AI_PROVIDER=
SAMI_AI_MODEL=
SAMI_AI_API_KEY=
SAMI_AI_BASE_URL=

For another OpenAI-compatible provider:

SAMI_AI_PROVIDER=custom
SAMI_AI_MODEL=<provider-model-id>
SAMI_AI_API_KEY=<provider-key>
SAMI_AI_BASE_URL=<openai-compatible-base-url>

Changing provider/model configuration never changes SaMi's tool,
permission, tenant, company, memory, confirmation, or audit boundaries.
